   $q$In a proof that $PA = PB$, line $\ell$ is perpendicular to $AB$ at its midpoint $M$ and point $P$ is on $\ell$, so $\angle PMA$ and $\angle PMB$ are both right angles. Which reason justifies Step 2, $\angle PMA \cong \angle PMB$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Alternate Interior Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-parallel-line-theorem-without-parallel-lines"},{"text":"Vertical Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-adjacent-with-vertical"},{"text":"All right angles are congruent","is_correct":true},{"text":"Definition of midpoint","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-a-segment-fact-for-an-angle"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$All right angles are congruent$q$, $q$Because $\ell \perp AB$, both angles measure $90^\circ$, and all right angles are congruent. The two angles share side $PM$ and are adjacent, so they are not vertical angles, and the midpoint definition is about the segments $AM$ and $MB$.$q$, 'draft', null)
