   $q$Lines $a$ and $b$ are cut by transversal $t$. Same-side interior angles measure $(4x + 8)^\circ$ and $(2x + 22)^\circ$. For what value of $x$ are lines $a$ and $b$ parallel?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$7$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"set-same-side-interior-angles-equal"},{"text":"$35$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-the-constant-instead-of-subtracting"},{"text":"$30$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-the-constants"},{"text":"$25$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$25$$q$, $q$By the converse of the Same-Side Interior Angles Theorem, the lines are parallel when the angles are supplementary: $(4x + 8) + (2x + 22) = 180$, so $6x + 30 = 180$, $6x = 150$, and $x = 25$. Setting the angles equal gives $x = 7$, but same-side interior angles must add to $180^\circ$.$q$, 'draft', null)
